{-
   The portable vector set the ownership resolver is built against - `lib/Core/Vector.yana` written
   small, on `prelude/Core/Core.yana`'s terms.

   Every declaration is the real one: the same name, the same head, the same attribute and a
   signature the real file would accept. Reading the two side by side is the whole of the
   maintenance, and this file ends by deletion when `-resolver ownership` compiles `lib/`.

   Declarations without bodies, all of them, because there is nothing a body could say: what a
   `Vec(a)` *is* depends on the target's register width, and each of these is one instruction or a
   short arrangement of them that `compiler/resolve_mir/intrinsic/expand_vector.cpp` builds at the
   call. None of them is ever a call in the IR.

   **What is not here is most of what a vector does.** An add of two vectors is `Num`'s `+`, the bit
   operations are `Bitwise`'s, a lanewise comparison and `select` are `Lanewise`'s below, and a
   conversion that keeps the lane count is `Widen`, `Narrow` or `Bitcast`. Every one of those is an
   *instance* the compiler generates over `Vec(a, n)` - `provideVectorInstances` - and none of them
   needed an instruction the IR did not already have, which is Design-Vector §3.2's claim tested.

   `vectorBytes`, the bulk operations of `Reduce.yana` and the CPU intrinsics of `Native/Intrinsic`
   are the parts of the real file this one leaves out: the first is a supplied global, and the other
   two are library code over containers this stage has not reached.
-}

{-
   Building a vector, reading one lane of it, and folding one down to a scalar - Design-Vector §3.3.

   `index` is a compile-time constant and the expansion says so: a runtime lane index is a shuffle on
   x86 and does not exist at all on some targets.

   The count is a const parameter wherever an *argument* supplies it. `Vec(a)` in a parameter is the
   target's natural width and nothing else, so without it `lane(v, 0)` on a `Vec(I16, 4)` would have
   no overload at all on a target whose natural count is eight.

   `splat`, `zero` and `iota` are const parameters too, with a **default** of zero. What decides
   their count is the result; what the default answers is the case where nothing does, and zero is
   already the natural form written down.
-}
@builtin(vecSplat) pub fn (n: Int = 0) splat(value: a) -> Vec(a, n)
@builtin(vecLane) pub fn (n: Int) lane(vector: Vec(a, n), index: Int) -> a
@builtin(vecWithLane) pub fn (n: Int) withLane(vector: Vec(a, n), index: Int, value: a) -> Vec(a, n)

{-
   The reductions, whose *order* is a stated language property rather than an implementation detail -
   Design-Vector §4.5. Each is the adjacent-pair tree `(a0+a1) + (a2+a3)`, on every target, which is
   what keeps a float sum from answering different bits under two vector widths of one backend.
-}
@builtin(vecHorizontalSum) pub fn (n: Int) horizontalSum(vector: Vec(a, n)) -> a
@builtin(vecHorizontalProduct) pub fn (n: Int) horizontalProduct(vector: Vec(a, n)) -> a
@builtin(vecHorizontalMin) pub fn (n: Int) horizontalMin(vector: Vec(a, n)) -> a
@builtin(vecHorizontalMax) pub fn (n: Int) horizontalMax(vector: Vec(a, n)) -> a

{-
   `zero` and `iota` take no argument at all and are selected by what the result is asked to be,
   which is the ordinary return-type selection - the lane *count* included, since the count is a
   const parameter with a default rather than the natural form and nothing else.

   `lanes` is the one that is not an instruction: it answers a number the *type* already carries, so
   the expansion is a constant and there is nothing left of the call.

   `abs` is the value with its sign removed, per lane.

   **`min`, `max` and `abs` are plain functions beside class members of the same name and arity**,
   which is `doc/spec/overloading.md`'s R1 exactly: an overload set is every class function of a
   `(name, arity)` plus at most one plain function. `Ord` declares `min` and `max` over two `a`s and
   `Signed` declares `abs` over one, and a vector joins neither class - a comparison of two vectors
   answers a *mask* rather than a `Bool`, so `Ord` is not what describes ordering them. So each of
   these is the plain half of a set whose other half is a class, and which one a call reaches is
   R5: the plain function wins where it matches, and a call at a scalar falls straight through to
   the class member because a `Vec(a, n)` position does not bind an `I32`.
-}
@builtin(vecZero) pub fn (n: Int = 0) zero() -> Vec(a, n)
@builtin(vecIota) pub fn (n: Int = 0) iota() -> Vec(a, n)
@builtin(vecLanes) pub fn (n: Int) lanes(vector: Vec(a, n)) -> Int

@builtin(vecAbs) pub fn (n: Int) abs(vector: Vec(a, n)) -> Vec(a, n)

@builtin(vecMin) pub fn (n: Int) min(lhs: Vec(a, n), rhs: Vec(a, n)) -> Vec(a, n)
@builtin(vecMax) pub fn (n: Int) max(lhs: Vec(a, n), rhs: Vec(a, n)) -> Vec(a, n)

{-
   The square root and the fused multiply-add, and the four roundings beside them.

   **One declaration each over an unconstrained `a`, covering the scalar and the vector both.**
   Nothing in this language had a square root at any width, so what §3.3 asks for over lanes is the
   thing a scalar wanted all along - `sqrt(x)` on a `Double` and `sqrt(v)` on a `Vec(Float)` are the
   same instruction over two types rather than two functions. `a` is unconstrained because the class
   that would bound it does not exist; what holds the argument to a float is the intrinsic's own
   diagnostic, at the call, naming the type.

   `fma` is `a * b + c` with **at most one rounding**, which is what makes it a function rather than
   two operators: the language says it *may* fuse, so a target with a fused instruction gives one
   rounding and a target without gives two, and a program that must not fuse writes `a * b + c`.

   `round` breaks a tie away from zero - `round(2.5)` is 3 - which is C's rule and not the even-tie
   rule an FPU's default mode uses.
-}
@builtin(vecSqrt) pub fn sqrt(value: a) -> a
@builtin(vecFma) pub fn fma(lhs: a, rhs: a, addend: a) -> a

@builtin(vecTrunc) pub fn trunc(value: a) -> a
@builtin(vecFloor) pub fn floor(value: a) -> a
@builtin(vecCeil) pub fn ceil(value: a) -> a
@builtin(vecRound) pub fn round(value: a) -> a

{-
   The conversions that change the lane *count*. The ones that keep it are `Widen`, `Narrow` and
   `Bitcast` over vector types and are not functions at all - Design-Vector §3.4.

   Each is a shuffle and a conversion, which is what a lane-count change is. What decides the result
   is the *call*, since nothing in the argument does: `unpackLow(v) :: Vec(I32)` is how one is
   written, and asking for a shape that is not half and whole of one width is reported at the call.
-}
@builtin(vecUnpackLow) pub fn unpackLow(vector: Vec(a)) -> Vec(b)
@builtin(vecUnpackHigh) pub fn unpackHigh(vector: Vec(a)) -> Vec(b)
@builtin(vecPackLanes) pub fn packLanes(lhs: Vec(a), rhs: Vec(a)) -> Vec(b)

{-
   A window of `n` lanes out of the two vectors placed end to end - x86's `palignr`, ARM's `vext`.

   `alignLanes(above, below, from)` is lane `from + i` of the sequence `below` followed by `above`,
   so `from = 0` answers `below` unchanged and `from = n` answers `above`. **The second operand is
   the *earlier* one**, which reads backwards until it is said out loud: `below` supplies the
   low-numbered lanes of the concatenation. That is the order x86's instruction has, and reversing it
   here would only put the difference somewhere less visible.

   `from` is a constant for `rotate`'s reason: the distance is the instruction's own immediate. A
   value at or past `2n` is refused rather than taken modulo anything, since unlike a rotation there
   is nothing for it to wrap around to.
-}
@builtin(vecAlignLanes) pub fn (n: Int) alignLanes(above: Vec(a, n), below: Vec(a, n), from: Int) -> Vec(a, n)

{-
   The general rearrangement - Design-Vector §3.3, Design-Swizzle §13.

   `shuffle(v, pattern)` answers lane `pattern[i]` of `v` in lane `i`, and `shuffle2` reads the same
   index against the two vectors placed end to end. **The pattern's length is the result's lane
   count**, which is what makes one declaration serve every width.

   The pattern is a **constant**, on `rotate`'s terms and for its reason: it is a field of the
   instruction on every target that has one. An entry naming a lane that is not there is refused
   where it is written.

   `reverse`, `rotate` and the interleaves are the named patterns of this, kept as their own names
   because a pattern built from the lane count cannot be written wrong at a call site and one written
   out can.
-}
@builtin(vecShuffle) pub fn (n: Int, m: Int) shuffle(vector: Vec(a, n), pattern: [Int *m]) -> Vec(a, m)
@builtin(vecShuffle2) pub fn (n: Int, m: Int) shuffle2(lhs: Vec(a, n), rhs: Vec(a, n), pattern: [Int *m]) -> Vec(a, m)

@builtin(vecReverse) pub fn (n: Int) reverse(vector: Vec(a, n)) -> Vec(a, n)
@builtin(vecRotate) pub fn (n: Int) rotate(vector: Vec(a, n), by: Int) -> Vec(a, n)
@builtin(vecInterleaveLow) pub fn (n: Int) interleaveLow(lhs: Vec(a, n), rhs: Vec(a, n)) -> Vec(a, n)
@builtin(vecInterleaveHigh) pub fn (n: Int) interleaveHigh(lhs: Vec(a, n), rhs: Vec(a, n)) -> Vec(a, n)

{-
   Comparing lanes, and choosing between two vectors by the answer - Design-Vector §3.2.

   A class rather than six functions, and the functional dependency `v -> m` is what earns it: `a .<
   b` has to infer its own result without an ascription, and one vector shape has exactly one mask
   shape.

   The dotted spellings are borrowed from Fortran and Julia and are ordinary declarable operators
   here - a symbol run beginning with `.` lexes as one operator, so nothing in the parser changes.
   They sit at the precedence of the comparisons they mirror, which is what makes `a .< b and c .< d`
   group the way `a < b and c < d` does.

   `select` is in the class rather than beside it because it is the only operation that relates a
   mask back to the vector it came from, and a class keyed on the pair is where that relation lives.

   **The member order is part of the interface**: the compiler reaches each by position, and
   `known.def`'s `lanewise` row is where that dependency is written down.
-}
infixl 3 .==
infixl 3 .!=
infixl 3 .<
infixl 3 .<=
infixl 3 .>
infixl 3 .>=

@builtin(lanewise)
pub class Lanewise(v -> m):
  fn .==(lhs: v, rhs: v) -> m
  fn .!=(lhs: v, rhs: v) -> m
  fn .<(lhs: v, rhs: v) -> m
  fn .<=(lhs: v, rhs: v) -> m
  fn .>(lhs: v, rhs: v) -> m
  fn .>=(lhs: v, rhs: v) -> m
  fn select(mask: m, ifTrue: v, ifFalse: v) -> v

{-
   Reading a mask - Design-Vector §3.2.

   `firstSet` is the one that makes a search loop terminate correctly, and it is why this family is
   portable rather than left to a platform module: without it, "which lane matched" is a movemask and
   a bit scan on one target and something else everywhere else. It answers the lane count when
   nothing is set, so `firstSet(m)` indexes a chunk without a preceding `any`.

   Masks get `and`, `or`, `xor` and `not` from `Bitwise`, whose instance the compiler generates - so
   the combining of two masks is written the way the combining of two conditions is.

   The lane count is a const parameter here for the reason it is one on `lane`: `Mask(a)` in a
   parameter is the target's natural width and nothing else, so without it a mask over a *written*
   width would have no reader at all.
-}
@builtin(maskAny) pub fn (n: Int) any(mask: Mask(a, n)) -> Bool
@builtin(maskAll) pub fn (n: Int) all(mask: Mask(a, n)) -> Bool
@builtin(maskNone) pub fn (n: Int) none(mask: Mask(a, n)) -> Bool
@builtin(maskCount) pub fn (n: Int) count(mask: Mask(a, n)) -> Int
@builtin(maskFirstSet) pub fn (n: Int) firstSet(mask: Mask(a, n)) -> Int

{-
   The first `count` lanes set and the rest clear - the tail mask, and the one operation in this
   family that is about a *length* rather than about a comparison somebody wrote.

   It is here rather than left to `iota() .< splat(count)` for a reason that is not brevity: written
   that way the count has to reach the lane type, and a lane narrower than an `Int` cannot be handed
   one. `Vec(I16)`'s tail mask is not expressible in source at all, and every wider one is
   expressible only because the conversion happens to widen.
-}
@builtin(maskUpTo) pub fn maskUpTo(count: Int) -> Mask(a)
